dency, and no amount of internal scaling can overcome an external rate limit.\n\n3. RETRY POLICY ASSESSMENT\n\nThe exponential-backoff retry policy with a maximum of three retries amplified the incident significantly. Retry volume increased by 420% at 09:16 (E06), just one minute after 429s first appeared. Each retried request consumed a worker slot for the full round-trip duration plus the backoff wait time, effectively tripling the load on dependency B for each original failed request. The policy lacked three critical safeguards: (a) a circuit-breaker to stop retrying when error rates exceed a threshold, (b) differentiation between retriable errors (5xx server errors, timeouts) and non-retriable errors (429 rate limits), and (c) a global retry budget to cap total amplification. Temporarily disabling retries at 09:26 caused queue depth to fall 38% in just six minutes (E08), providing direct experimental proof that retries were the primary amplifier. The trade-off was an increase in direct failure rate, which is a more honest failure mode than a hung queue and can be handled by upstream callers.\n\n4. RECOVERY SEQUENCE AND CAUSAL CONFIRMATION\n\nAt 09:35, dependency B rate limits were raised and HTTP 429 responses fell below 1%; queue depth then returned below 150 within eleven minutes (E09). This recovery sequence confirms the causal chain: rate-limit removal eliminated the stimulus for retries, which unblocked workers, which allowed the queue to drain at normal processing speed. The tight temporal correlation between rate-limit increase and queue normalization (eleven minutes) is consistent with workers being blocked on retries rather than being compute-bound. Furthermore, no deploy, schema change, host restart, network route change, or credential rotation occurred between 08:00 and 10:00 (E010), definitively ruling out internal platform changes as a root cause.\n\n5. ALTERNATIVE HYPOTHESIS EVALUATION\n\nAn alternative hypothesis is that a resource exhaustion even