dency, and no amount of internal scaling can overcome an external rate limit.\n\n3. RETRY POLICY ASSESSMENT\n\nThe exponential-backoff retry policy with a maximum of three retries amplified the incident significantly. Retry volume increased by 420% at 09:16 (E06), just one minute after 429s first appeared. Each retried request consumed a worker slot for the full round-trip duration plus the backoff wait time, effectively tripling the load on dependency B for each original failed request. The policy lacked three critical safeguards: (a) a circuit-breaker to stop retrying when error rates exceed a threshold, (b) differentiation between retriable errors (5xx server errors, timeouts) and non-retriable errors (429 rate limits), and (c) a global retry budget to cap total amplification. Temporarily disabling retries at 09:26 caused queue depth to fall 38% in just six minutes (E08), providing direct experimental proof that retries were the primary amplifier. The trade-off was an increase in direct failure rate, which is a more honest failure mode than a hung queue and can be handled by upstream callers.\n\n4. RECOVERY SEQUENCE AND CAUSAL CONFIRMATION\n\nAt 09:35, dependency B rate limits were raised and HTTP 429 responses fell below 1%; queue depth then returned below 150 within eleven minutes (E09). This recovery sequence confirms the causal chain: rate-limit removal eliminated the stimulus for retries, which unblocked workers, which allowed the queue to drain at normal processing speed. The tight temporal correlation between rate-limit increase and queue normalization (eleven minutes) is consistent with workers being blocked on retries rather than being compute-bound. Furthermore, no deploy, schema change, host restart, network route change, or credential rotation occurred between 08:00 and 10:00 (E10), definitively ruling out internal platform changes as a root cause.\n\n5. ALTERNATIVE HYPOTHESIS EVALUATION\n\nAn alternative hypothesis is that a resource exhaustion event (CPU, memory, or disk) on the workers themselves caused tasks to slow, deepening the queue. This hypothesis is weakly supported by the evidence for two reasons. First, CPU was only 43%, memory was 51%, and disk was 22% at 09:14 (E04), all well below saturation. Second, if resource exhaustion were the cause, adding workers at 09:20 should have produced a meaningful queue reduction by distributing the load across more compute capacity. Instead, the addition of 8 workers achieved only a 6% reduction (E07), which is inconsistent with an internal resource bottleneck. The evidence strongly favors the external-dependency hypothesis over internal resource exhaustion.\n\n6. LATENCY ANALYSIS\n\nMedian task latency rose from 1.8 seconds to 14.2 seconds at 09:12 (E03), an approximately 8x increase. This latency spike preceded the explicit 429 measurements at 09:15, suggesting that dependency B was already throttling requests before returning explicit 429 codes. The throttling may have manifested as increased response times (slow rejects) before the rate-limit policy switched to fast rejects with 429 status codes. This is a common pattern in rate-limiting systems where the initial response to overload is queuing and delay before explicit rejection.\n\n7. CONFIDENCE ASSESSMENT\n\nThe primary-cause attribution to dependency B rate limiting carries high confidence (92%) given the tight temporal correlation, the recovery upon rate-limit increase, and the disconfirmation of internal causes. The amplifier attribution to the retry policy carries very high confidence (96%) given the 38% queue reduction observed upon retry disablement (E08). The lower confidence on F07 (78%) reflects the inference that dependency B was throttling before explicit 429s appeared, which is plausible but not directly proven by the evidence. Overall, the causal narrative is well-supported by multiple independent observations converging on the same conclusion.\n\n8. SYNTHESIS AND CONCLUSION\n\nThe incident follows a classic retry-storm pattern: an external dependency degradation (rate limiting by dependency B) triggered a well-intentioned retry policy that amplified the load on the already-struggling dependency, creating a feedback loop that sustained the queue backlog long after the initial trigger. The system exhibited three failure modes simultaneously: (1) insufficient backpressure from the dependency (no early signaling before 429s), (2) insufficient protection in the client (no circuit breaker, no retry budget), and (3) insufficient observability (no alert on queue depth or dependency error rate before latency spiked). The staged remediation plan should address all three layers: client-side circuit breakers and retry budgets to prevent amplification, dependency-level rate-limit negotiation to prevent recurrence, and improved alerting to enable earlier intervention. The total incident duration was approximately 46 minutes (09:00 to approximately 09:46 when queue depth normalized), with the critical amplification phase lasting from 09:15 to 09:35 (20 minutes). The window for early intervention was between 09:10 (when queue depth spiked) and 09:15 (when 429s appeared), a five-minute window that could have been exploited with automated circuit-breaking.",
